{"findings":[{"parameter":"insulation.material","claim":"XLPE cables have a standard maximum conductor operating temperature of 90 °C, above which thermal degradation of the insulation is accelerated.","relevance":"Sets the thermal ceiling that governs ampacity calculations and aging rate for an XLPE-insulated 11 kV cable operating in a hot-climate underground environment.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590123023006795","title":"Enhancing medium voltage underground circuit design: Assessing limitations, thermal influence, and accurate modelling - ScienceDirect","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":"2023-10-29","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"high"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"IEC 60287-1 provides the standard framework for underground MV cable current-rating design but does not give specific guidance for desert conditions with high soil thermal resistance, nor does it include dynamic effects such as wind or vibration.","relevance":"Directly relevant to a hot-climate underground 11 kV installation, where standard IEC rating assumptions may not fully capture desert/high-ambient-temperature soil behavior.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590123023006795","title":"Enhancing medium voltage underground circuit design: Assessing limitations, thermal influence, and accurate modelling - ScienceDirect","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":"2023-10-29","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"IEC 60287-2 assumes a steady-state, homogeneous soil/backfill thermal environment and does not account for variations in soil thermal properties or temperature profiles found in desert conditions, nor does it include thermal cycling stress.","relevance":"Indicates a known limitation of the governing thermal-resistance standard when applied to hot-climate soils, relevant to sizing an 11 kV XLPE cable for underground distribution.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590123023006795","title":"Enhancing medium voltage underground circuit design: Assessing limitations, thermal influence, and accurate modelling - ScienceDirect","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":"2023-10-29","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"A case study found that for an XLPE 630 mm² cable, increasing soil thermal resistivity from 1.5 K·m/W to 4.892 K·m/W significantly reduced ampacity, and in a specific high-thermal-resistivity pit condition current capacity was reduced to 62.2% of the value under normal (1.5 K·m/W, 25 °C) conditions.", "relevance":"Quantifies how dry/high-resistivity soils, common in hot arid climates, can substantially cut the deliverable current of underground XLPE distribution cables relative to standard assumptions.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590123023006795","title":"Enhancing medium voltage underground circuit design: Assessing limitations, thermal influence, and accurate modelling - ScienceDirect","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":"2023-10-29","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"IEC 60502-2 covers the design, dimensions and testing requirements for single- and three-core extruded-insulation power cables (including XLPE) rated up to 36 kV, which encompasses 11 kV three-core distribution cables.","relevance":"Identifies the governing construction/test standard applicable to a 3-core 11 kV XLPE copper underground distribution cable.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/steel-armor-wire","title":"Steel Armor Wire - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":null,"dateBasis":"undated","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"Ampacity of MV underground cables is normally evaluated using the IEC 60287 series (current-rating formulae, thermal-resistance formulae, and operating-condition sections), and these ratings are a function of ambient temperature and soil thermal resistivity.","relevance":"Establishes the standard methodology by which the current rating of the 11 kV XLPE copper cable would be derived, with ambient (soil) temperature as a direct input relevant to a hot climate.","url":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8240508","title":"MV underground cables: Effects of soil thermal resistivity on anomalous working temperatures | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore","publisher":"IEEE","publishedOn":null,"dateBasis":"undated","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"CIGRE notes that current-rating calculations depend heavily on soil thermal parameters (density, moisture content) and ambient temperature, which often vary along a route and are only known to limited accuracy, making the quality of input data a key source of rating uncertainty.","relevance":"Highlights that in hot/arid climates, where soil moisture and temperature can vary significantly and dry out under load, the accuracy of the underlying soil data strongly affects the calculated ampacity of the underground cable system.","url":"https://electra.cigre.org/324-october-2022/technical-brochures/power-cable-rating-examples-for-calculation-tool-verification.html","title":"Power Cable Rating Examples for Calculation Tool Verification | ELECTRA","publisher":"CIGRE","publishedOn":"2022-10","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"insulation.material","claim":"Experiments cited in a thermal assessment study found that the critical temperature for dry-zone formation around a buried cable is close to 60 °C for wet soils, and the IEC 60287-1 ampacity method accounting for soil drying-out was found to be the most accurate with least risk of exceeding the maximum permissible cable temperature.","relevance":"Relevant to a hot-climate underground 11 kV XLPE cable because soil drying-out around the cable (common at high ambient/soil temperatures) raises local thermal resistance and can push conductor temperature toward or above the XLPE limit if not accounted for.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1359431116321160","title":"Thermal assessment of sheathed medium voltage power cables under non-sinusoidal current and daily load cycle - ScienceDirect","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":"2017-05-17","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"A study on underground power cables states that all the international standards considered limit the guidance to only two circuits in the same trench, which is noted as a limitation for denser underground layouts.","relevance":"Relevant if the 3-core 11 kV cable is installed alongside other circuits in a hot-climate trench, since mutual heating beyond two-circuit scenarios is not directly covered by current standard guidance.","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2590123023006795","title":"Enhancing medium voltage underground circuit design: Assessing limitations, thermal influence, and accurate modelling - ScienceDirect","publisher":"ScienceDirect","publishedOn":"2023-10-29","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"},{"parameter":"general","claim":"IEC 60502-1:2021 specifies construction, dimensions and test requirements for power cables with extruded solid insulation for distribution network and industrial fixed installations, within its covered voltage range.","relevance":"Serves as part of the governing IEC standard series for extruded (XLPE) power cables, establishing baseline construction/testing requirements applicable to the broader 60502 family covering up to 36 kV including 11 kV three-core cables.","url":"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/64963","title":"IEC 60502-1:2021 | IEC","publisher":"IEC","publishedOn":"2021","dateBasis":"stated_on_page","confidence":"medium"}]}
